The issue is that Google Chat's webhook API doesn't support full Markdown — it only supports a limited subset. Your formatForGoogleChat function is on the right track but isn't catching everything.
Here are the specific problems:

#### headers aren't being caught — your regex only handles # and ##, but the AI-generated text likely includes ### and #### too.
Nested or inline bold (**...**) is being converted to single *...*, but if there's already a * adjacent or the text has edge cases (like **text**: or **text.**), the regex fails.
Bullet points with sub-levels (like  -) aren't matched by ^- .
Code blocks or other Markdown artifacts from Gemini's output slip through untouched.

Here's the corrected function:
typescriptconst formatForGoogleChat = (text: string): string => {
  return text
    // Headers: any level (####, ###, ##, #) → bold text, no symbols
    .replace(/^#{1,6}\s*(.*?)$/gm, '*$1*')

    // Bold: **text** → *text* (handles punctuation adjacent)
    .replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '*$1*')

    // Bullet points: any indentation level
    .replace(/^[\t ]*[-*]\s/gm, '• ')

    // Numbered lists: keep but clean indentation
    .replace(/^[\t ]*(\d+)\.\s/gm, '$1. ')

    // Remove horizontal rules
    .replace(/^[-_*]{3,}$/gm, '──────────────────')

    // Remove leftover triple backticks (code blocks)
    .replace(/```[\w]*\n?/g, '')

    // Collapse multiple blank lines into one
    .replace(/\n{3,}/g, '\n\n');
};
Key changes:
/^#{1,6}\s*(.*?)$/gm — catches all header levels (# through ######) in one regex instead of handling them one by one. Google Chat will just show the text in bold, which is the closest equivalent.
/^[\t ]*[-*]\s/gm — the [\t ]* prefix catches indented bullets too (sub-lists), and [-*] catches both - and * used as bullet markers.

